        -:    4:// Why this exists. Peak RSS from getrusage is a single number for the whole
        -:    5:// process, so it can establish that the Rust build holds more memory than the
        -:    6:// C++ build without saying what the extra is. Section 7.5.4 records exactly that
        -:    7:// gap: the split between arena pre-reservation, per-thread caches, and static
        -:    8:// footprint is left unresolved. jemalloc already tracks that split internally.
        -:    9://
        -:   10:// This is not the heap profile that bench/alloc_profile.sh collects. That needs a
        -:   11:// prof-enabled build and jeprof to symbolise, and attributes bytes to allocation
        -:   12:// sites; this needs neither and attributes bytes to allocator structures. They
        -:   13:// answer different halves of the same question, and this half runs anywhere.
        -:   14://
        -:   15:// Both languages read the same six counters from the same allocator -- the C++
        -:   16:// side through mallctl here, the Rust side through tikv-jemalloc-ctl -- so the
        -:   17:// numbers can be differenced. Using two allocator-specific tools instead would
        -:   18:// reproduce the mismatched-instrument problem that alloc_profile.sh was written
        -:   19:// to avoid.
        -:   20://
        -:   21:// mallctl is declared weak rather than included from <jemalloc/jemalloc.h>: the
        -:   22:// runtime ships in libjemalloc.so.2 while the header is a separate dev package,
